  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Giving a reason",
     "texto": [
      "**-(으)니까** gives the reason or basis for what follows: **날씨가 좋으니까 공원에 갈까요?** = since the weather is nice, shall we go to the park? It also sounds natural before a request, order or suggestion. It does not mean the reason has to be obvious to the listener."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "How it is formed",
     "texto": [
      "With a stem ending in a vowel or **ㄹ**, add **-니까**: 가다 → **가니까**, 살다 → **사니까**. With another consonant, add **-으니까**: 먹다 → **먹으니까**, 좋다 → **좋으니까**. With **ㄹ**, the stem is kept without the ㄹ: 만들다 → **만드니까**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Compared with -아/어서",
     "texto": [
      "Both forms can express cause. **-아/어서** often presents a cause-and-result link; **-(으)니까** stresses the reason given by the speaker and fits especially well before a suggestion, an order or a request. The difference depends on context; it is not simply «obvious fact» versus «non-obvious fact»."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
